## File-level checklist (agent must satisfy on touched files)

When you edit a file, ensure:

- [ ] Class/struct has a `/// <summary>` (2–5 sentences: role, world, reads/writes, partners).
- [ ] Public and non-obvious private methods have `/// <summary>` (and params if not obvious).
- [ ] Methods longer than ~10 lines have section headers or block comments for each phase.
- [ ] Non-trivial logic inside methods has inline comments with **what + why**.
- [ ] Domain terms are explained on first use in that file or method.
- [ ] At least one `[TITAN-ORBIT]` or `[STANDARD]`/`[NETCODE]`/`[ECS/DOTS]` tag where the distinction matters.
- [ ] Cross-references to paired systems or rules when behavior depends on pipeline order.

---

## Do not

- Delete existing teaching comments unless they are wrong — update them.
- Write comments that only repeat the code (`// increment i`).
- Assume the reader knows NetCode, Burst, ghost fields, or toroidal map math.
- Hide "this is weird but intentional" — say so and tag `[TITAN-ORBIT]`.
- Leave large uncommented blocks in sim, networking, or movement code.

---

## Priority files (extra depth)

Apply **maximum** comment depth when editing:

- `ECS/Systems/**` — movement, physics, shooting, combat, orbit, RPC handlers
- `ECS/Components/**` — especially ghost-serialized fields
- `Networking/**`, `*GhostAuthoring.cs`, bootstrap / session code
- Anything under `titan-orbit-ship-simulation.mdc` invariants

Presentation-only UI/VFX may use slightly fewer inline comments, but file and method summaries still required if behavior is non-obvious.
